<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oirats</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oirat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oirats are a group of western Mongol peoples who became a major power on the Mongolian and Central Asian steppe from the fifteenth century. They raided the eastern lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, the successor state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a>. Their pressure contributed to the decline of the Chagatai khans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oirats.jpg" alt="Oirats" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bukar', Michail · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bukar%27,_Michail_-_Ehepaar_aus_dem_Volksstamm_der_Kalmyken_in_ihrer_Nationaltracht_(Zeno_Fotografie).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Oirats, also called Oirds and formerly Eluts or Eleuths, are the westernmost group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, whose ancestral home lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, a 13th-century chronicle, counts them among the forest people and says they lived under a shaman-chief known as bäki. They fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> in his early rise but were defeated, and they submitted fully after the death of his rival Jumukha. Historically they comprised four major tribes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorbet">Dörbet</a> and Khoshut, and the name may derive from Dörben Öörd, meaning the Allied Four.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> the Oirats reconvened as a loose alliance and gradually spread eastwards into lands held by the Eastern Mongols. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> helped their rise after 1410. The ruler who led them furthest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esen-taishi">Esen Taishi</a>, led them from 1438 to 1454 and unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> under his puppet khan Taisun Khan. In 1449 his cavalry destroyed Ming defences at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> and captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengtong-emperor">Zhengtong Emperor</a> at Tumu, whom Esen returned the following year after a failed ransom attempt. Esen was killed soon after claiming the title of khan, and Oirat power declined.</p><p>The Oirats converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> around 1615. In 1637 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> of the Khoshuts defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> prince at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school and in the early 1640s was proclaimed Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a>, founding the Khoshut Khanate. In the 17th century the Dzungar Khanate, ruled by Choros noblemen, arose in the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> in the 1750s, and some scholars estimate that about 80 per cent of the Dzungar population, which had reached 600,000 in 1755, was wiped out in the conquest of 1755–1757.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1607 Oirats led by Kho Orlok of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghuts</a> and Dalai Tayishi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorbet">Dörbets</a> moved west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and founded the Kalmyk Khanate, and by 1630 had reached the steppes of southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. They came under Russian control in 1724. In January 1771 about 170,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a> left the Volga for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubashi-khan">Ubashi Khan</a>. The modern Kalmyks of Kalmykia are Oirats. In the 17th century the Khoshut monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaya-pandita">Zaya Pandita</a> devised the Clear Script for the Oirat language, still used by some Oirats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oirats” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moghulistan lasted in this diminished form for more than a century, its khans often dominated by the Dughlat amirs, and withstood Timurid invasions from the west and <b>Oirat</b> raids from the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dzungars were the leading tribe of the <b>Oirats</b>, the western Mongols, who had formed a loose confederation known as the Four Oirat after the fall of the Yuan dynasty and in the fifteenth century, under Esen Taishi, briefly dominated Mongolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The khans fought the Shaybanids of Bukhara for the towns of the Syr Darya, the Nogais to the west and the Moghuls and <b>Oirats</b> to the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kazakh-khanate">Kazakh Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ming Great Wall seen by visitors today is largely a 16th-century brick-faced work, built after the Zhengtong Emperor was captured by the <b>Oirats</b> at Tumu in 1449.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>No one doubts that the Khalkha of Mongolia is Mongolian, but <b>Oirat</b> (with Kalmyk), Buryat and Ordos may be dialects of it or separate languages.</blockquote><a href="/language/mongolian">Mongolian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>The last great steppe empire was the Dzungar Khanate of the <b>Oirat</b> Mongols, founded in the 1630s.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
